1
00:00:25,826 --> 00:00:28,125
♪♪

2
00:00:36,370 --> 00:00:38,305
♪♪

3
00:00:49,349 --> 00:00:51,341
♪♪

4
00:01:21,315 --> 00:01:23,307
♪♪

5
00:01:53,347 --> 00:01:55,339
♪♪

6
00:02:33,887 --> 00:02:36,254
I hate the way men look at you.

7
00:02:38,125 --> 00:02:42,271
If men stop looking at me, I
don't pay my rent, Reverend.

8
00:02:42,295 --> 00:02:44,140
We should've met at your place.

9
00:02:44,164 --> 00:02:45,441
This was your choice.

10
00:02:45,465 --> 00:02:47,477
Just remember,
my fee is the same

11
00:02:47,501 --> 00:02:48,799
whether it's here or there.

12
00:02:51,805 --> 00:02:54,070
I think our time's
just about up anyway.

13
00:03:01,314 --> 00:03:02,659
Always a pleasure, Reverend.

14
00:03:02,683 --> 00:03:05,261
May I see you next week?

15
00:03:05,285 --> 00:03:07,811
You know where to reach me.

16
00:03:27,941 --> 00:03:29,986
<i>No, Tuesday's no good.</i>

17
00:03:30,010 --> 00:03:33,003
How about... Wednesday at 7:00?

18
00:03:35,182 --> 00:03:37,827
David Campbell,
how can you say that?

19
00:03:37,851 --> 00:03:40,496
You know you're my favorite.

20
00:03:41,688 --> 00:03:43,766
You bet I'll prove it.

21
00:03:43,790 --> 00:03:45,101
Hey, did I tell you...

22
00:03:45,125 --> 00:03:48,271
Um, I listened to your
radio show the other day.

23
00:03:48,295 --> 00:03:49,763
You give good advice, Doctor.

24
00:03:53,266 --> 00:03:55,411
7:00 Wednesday.

25
00:03:55,435 --> 00:03:56,926
Bye.

26
00:04:15,489 --> 00:04:18,067
Had I known you were this
good, I would've voted for you.

27
00:04:19,826 --> 00:04:22,819
What a man has to do
today to win one lousy vote.

28
00:04:24,931 --> 00:04:25,908
Jeffrey.

29
00:04:25,932 --> 00:04:27,610
Hmm?

30
00:04:27,634 --> 00:04:31,114
I need your help with a
little problem I'm having.

31
00:04:31,138 --> 00:04:33,216
Oh?

32
00:04:33,240 --> 00:04:34,383
What's that?

33
00:04:37,577 --> 00:04:39,622
Anything.

34
00:04:39,646 --> 00:04:41,023
You know Ann Rawls?

35
00:04:41,047 --> 00:04:42,325
Yeah.

36
00:04:42,349 --> 00:04:44,627
She's, uh... what do
they call her nowadays?

37
00:04:44,651 --> 00:04:45,846
Uh, your madam.

38
00:04:47,554 --> 00:04:49,599
I may be wrong,
but I don't think

39
00:04:49,623 --> 00:04:51,567
she wants me working
for her anymore.

40
00:04:52,893 --> 00:04:55,438
Why would she want to
shoot herself in the foot?

41
00:04:55,462 --> 00:04:58,107
You're the best she's got.

42
00:04:58,131 --> 00:05:01,077
Yeah, well, um, I haven't
exactly been straight with her.

43
00:05:01,101 --> 00:05:02,912
I mean, there have
been a few clients

44
00:05:02,936 --> 00:05:04,347
I haven't told her about.

45
00:05:04,371 --> 00:05:07,617
So? She's not the
only game in town.

46
00:05:07,641 --> 00:05:10,052
No, but she's the
best game in town.

47
00:05:10,076 --> 00:05:12,636
Most money, the best clients.

48
00:05:14,247 --> 00:05:16,341
You will talk to her,
won't you, Jeffrey?

49
00:05:18,251 --> 00:05:22,198
Well, um... Janie, I'm sorry.

50
00:05:22,222 --> 00:05:24,817
I, uh... I can't do that.

51
00:05:26,993 --> 00:05:28,371
I'm a state senator.

52
00:05:28,395 --> 00:05:31,627
I, uh... I can't get involved
in anything like that.

53
00:05:33,834 --> 00:05:36,360
I was hoping you
wouldn't disappoint me.

54
00:05:41,074 --> 00:05:43,586
Janie?

55
00:05:43,610 --> 00:05:46,170
What is that supposed to mean?

56
00:05:49,583 --> 00:05:52,451
You have no idea how good
you sound on tape, Senator.

57
00:05:54,154 --> 00:05:55,965
What the hell are
you talking about?

58
00:05:55,989 --> 00:05:58,234
You know those little playful
telephone conversations

59
00:05:58,258 --> 00:05:59,468
we've been having?

60
00:05:59,492 --> 00:06:03,105
I've recorded them...
just in case you needed

61
00:06:03,129 --> 00:06:05,975
a little motivation.

62
00:06:05,999 --> 00:06:08,077
<i>Senator, let me
ask you a question.</i>

63
00:06:08,101 --> 00:06:10,179
<i>If one of your
people stole from you,</i>

64
00:06:10,203 --> 00:06:11,914
<i>wouldn't you get rid of them?</i>

65
00:06:11,938 --> 00:06:14,183
Ann, I'm not condoning
what Janie did.

66
00:06:14,207 --> 00:06:17,019
I'm here to ask you
for a personal favor.

67
00:06:17,043 --> 00:06:18,855
Give her another chance, huh?

68
00:06:18,879 --> 00:06:20,223
I can't, Senator.

69
00:06:20,247 --> 00:06:22,258
I just don't trust her.

70
00:06:22,282 --> 00:06:26,295
Besides, it would set a bad
example for my other girls.

71
00:06:26,319 --> 00:06:27,548
Ann?

72
00:06:29,990 --> 00:06:31,267
Hello, Janie.

73
00:06:31,291 --> 00:06:34,103
Janie, I did what I could.

74
00:06:34,127 --> 00:06:35,605
Her mind is made up.

75
00:06:35,629 --> 00:06:37,406
Now, I want that tape.

76
00:06:37,430 --> 00:06:39,008
Good-bye, Jeffrey.

77
00:06:39,032 --> 00:06:41,577
Don't force my hand!

78
00:06:41,601 --> 00:06:44,264
Maybe you should
go to the police.

79
00:06:50,543 --> 00:06:52,989
It's quite something.

80
00:06:53,013 --> 00:06:56,592
Your clients have been
parading in here all day

81
00:06:56,616 --> 00:06:59,428
trying to get me
to change my mind.

82
00:06:59,452 --> 00:07:01,664
First, David Campbell,

83
00:07:01,688 --> 00:07:04,700
your radio psychologist
friend, dropped by.

84
00:07:04,724 --> 00:07:08,738
Then I got a visit
from Reverend Phelps.

85
00:07:08,762 --> 00:07:10,506
They both say

86
00:07:10,530 --> 00:07:14,297
you taped all of your
conversations with them.

87
00:07:16,169 --> 00:07:18,147
You're a bitch, Janie.

88
00:07:18,171 --> 00:07:20,816
I've made you a
lot of money, Ann.

89
00:07:20,840 --> 00:07:22,852
You're fired, Janie.

90
00:07:22,876 --> 00:07:25,554
I have your entire
client list, Ann.

91
00:07:25,578 --> 00:07:27,123
I'll give it to the press.

92
00:07:27,147 --> 00:07:28,658
Get out of here, Janie.

93
00:07:28,682 --> 00:07:31,914
There won't be a man in
Atlanta who'll touch your girls.

94
00:07:34,187 --> 00:07:38,234
<i>Why don't you give
me about 30 minutes?</i>

95
00:07:39,726 --> 00:07:42,238
I'm gonna take a bubble bath,

96
00:07:42,262 --> 00:07:44,140
put on some lotion.

97
00:07:47,567 --> 00:07:50,980
You are one naughty boy, Roger.

98
00:07:51,004 --> 00:07:52,982
30 minutes.

99
00:07:53,006 --> 00:07:54,998
Bye.

100
00:07:56,609 --> 00:07:58,601
Jerk.

101
00:08:27,240 --> 00:08:29,232
♪♪

102
00:09:01,875 --> 00:09:04,140
♪♪

103
00:09:05,178 --> 00:09:07,079
How did you get in here?

104
00:09:11,251 --> 00:09:13,095
What are you doing?

105
00:09:13,119 --> 00:09:14,630
No! No!

106
00:09:24,964 --> 00:09:26,742
Oh.

107
00:09:58,498 --> 00:10:00,776
Janie?

108
00:10:00,800 --> 00:10:02,311
Come on, Janie. It's Ann.

109
00:10:02,335 --> 00:10:04,531
I want to talk to you.

110
00:10:11,010 --> 00:10:13,002
Janie?

111
00:10:14,013 --> 00:10:15,413
Janie?

112
00:10:38,037 --> 00:10:39,835
Oh.

113
00:10:42,075 --> 00:10:44,353
Hey, Janie!

114
00:10:45,645 --> 00:10:47,223
Sugar?

115
00:10:47,247 --> 00:10:49,978
Come on, sugar.
What are you doing?

116
00:10:56,055 --> 00:10:58,767
<i>Why did you go to
Janie's apartment</i>

117
00:10:58,791 --> 00:11:00,569
in the first place?

118
00:11:00,593 --> 00:11:02,838
- Well, earlier in the day...
- Mm-hmm.

119
00:11:02,862 --> 00:11:05,274
She had threatened to
turn my girls' client list

120
00:11:05,298 --> 00:11:07,543
over to the press.

121
00:11:07,567 --> 00:11:10,579
- That would have put me out of business.
- Oh.

122
00:11:10,603 --> 00:11:14,049
I was going to try and
talk some sense into her.

123
00:11:14,073 --> 00:11:15,885
Maybe even give
her another chance.

124
00:11:15,909 --> 00:11:17,052
Mm-hmm.

125
00:11:17,076 --> 00:11:19,054
Uh, Lieutenant Brooks mentioned,

126
00:11:19,078 --> 00:11:23,893
uh, an-an audio tape
that Janie had made.

127
00:11:23,917 --> 00:11:25,227
What was that all about?

128
00:11:25,251 --> 00:11:26,729
Oh, Janie was
putting the pressure

129
00:11:26,753 --> 00:11:28,030
on three of her clients

130
00:11:28,054 --> 00:11:30,733
to try and convince
me not to get rid of her.

131
00:11:30,757 --> 00:11:32,334
Each of them paid me a visit.

132
00:11:32,358 --> 00:11:35,271
Oh. Oh, oh. But they didn't...
they didn't convince you?

133
00:11:35,295 --> 00:11:38,107
I'm a businesswoman,
Mr. Matlock.

134
00:11:38,131 --> 00:11:39,608
Uh-huh.

135
00:11:39,632 --> 00:11:42,378
Janie lied and stole from me.

136
00:11:42,402 --> 00:11:44,113
I had to fire her.

137
00:11:44,137 --> 00:11:45,281
Uh-huh.

138
00:11:45,305 --> 00:11:47,616
Yeah, well...

139
00:11:47,640 --> 00:11:49,385
What was... What
was on the tape?

140
00:11:49,409 --> 00:11:51,387
Well, apparently,

141
00:11:51,411 --> 00:11:55,224
she had taped several
explicit conversations

142
00:11:55,248 --> 00:11:57,259
with each of them

143
00:11:57,283 --> 00:12:00,296
that would prove embarrassing
if they were made public.

144
00:12:00,320 --> 00:12:04,300
Oh, I see. I see. Boy...

145
00:12:04,324 --> 00:12:08,819
it sure would be helpful
to get hold of that tape.

146
00:12:10,096 --> 00:12:13,624
Does that mean you're
going to take the case?

147
00:12:16,903 --> 00:12:18,881
Yeah, I think I will.

148
00:12:21,241 --> 00:12:22,241
Thank you.

149
00:12:24,877 --> 00:12:26,422
Has anyone ever told you

150
00:12:26,446 --> 00:12:30,850
that you have the most
piercing blue eyes?

151
00:12:32,018 --> 00:12:36,765
Uh, n-n-no, no,
I-I don't think so.

152
00:12:36,789 --> 00:12:39,535
Tell you what.

153
00:12:39,559 --> 00:12:42,037
Let's you and I
have dinner tonight.

154
00:12:42,061 --> 00:12:44,640
Well, you're in jail.

155
00:12:44,664 --> 00:12:47,042
Well, you'll take care of that.

156
00:12:47,066 --> 00:12:49,812
I'll pay the bail

157
00:12:49,836 --> 00:12:53,749
and then we can celebrate
our getting together.

158
00:12:53,773 --> 00:12:57,386
Well, uh... no, no,
no-no-not tonight.

159
00:12:57,410 --> 00:13:00,689
I mean, I've got a lot of work
to do... I mean, a lot of work.

160
00:13:00,713 --> 00:13:04,493
Oh, what a shame.

161
00:13:04,517 --> 00:13:06,362
Well, perhaps another time.

162
00:13:06,386 --> 00:13:07,496
Hmm.

163
00:13:07,520 --> 00:13:09,131
When you're not working.

164
00:13:10,456 --> 00:13:11,634
À bientôt.

165
00:13:11,658 --> 00:13:13,569
Uh, ma'am?

166
00:13:13,593 --> 00:13:14,970
It's French.

167
00:13:14,994 --> 00:13:18,641
It means "I'll be seeing you."

168
00:13:18,665 --> 00:13:23,612
Oh, yeah, well... à
bientôt to you, too.

169
00:13:32,779 --> 00:13:33,822
À bientôt.

170
00:13:42,121 --> 00:13:44,166
<i>Anybody figure out
how the killer got in?</i>

171
00:13:44,190 --> 00:13:45,467
<i>Probably had a key.</i>

172
00:13:45,491 --> 00:13:47,936
<i>Ann said Janie
had lost her keys.</i>

173
00:13:52,265 --> 00:13:53,475
Huh.

174
00:13:53,499 --> 00:13:56,478
Mm-hmm.

175
00:13:56,502 --> 00:13:58,547
I tell you what, Conrad...

176
00:13:58,571 --> 00:14:02,117
You find that tape and
I'll buy you a fine dinner.

177
00:14:02,141 --> 00:14:04,119
- Anywhere?
- Anywhere.

178
00:14:04,143 --> 00:14:06,922
Now I'm motivated.

179
00:14:06,946 --> 00:14:08,624
Yeah.

180
00:14:08,648 --> 00:14:12,961
Nice, fat New York
steak smothered in onions,

181
00:14:12,985 --> 00:14:15,831
baked potato with
sour cream and chives.

182
00:14:15,855 --> 00:14:17,833
How'd you two get in here?

183
00:14:17,857 --> 00:14:21,937
Oh, I... uh, the police
gave us this key.

184
00:14:21,961 --> 00:14:25,674
Uh, I'm representing the woman
accused of killing Janie Ladd.

185
00:14:25,698 --> 00:14:27,009
I'm Ben Matlock.

186
00:14:27,033 --> 00:14:28,177
Uh-huh, and you?

187
00:14:28,201 --> 00:14:29,244
Conrad McMasters.

188
00:14:29,268 --> 00:14:30,245
And you?

189
00:14:30,269 --> 00:14:31,246
Tom Marshall.

190
00:14:31,270 --> 00:14:32,247
I'm the super.

191
00:14:32,271 --> 00:14:33,582
I manage the building.

192
00:14:33,606 --> 00:14:34,650
I'm just checking
out a few things,

193
00:14:34,674 --> 00:14:35,718
seeing what needs to be done

194
00:14:35,742 --> 00:14:37,052
before the owners
can rent it again.

195
00:14:37,076 --> 00:14:39,888
Oh... we're looking
around a little ourselves.

196
00:14:39,912 --> 00:14:41,490
Okay, well, suit yourself.

197
00:14:41,514 --> 00:14:42,725
Yeah, I'm gonna
check the bedroom.

198
00:14:42,749 --> 00:14:44,326
Yeah.

199
00:14:44,350 --> 00:14:47,096
Did, uh, did you
know Janie Ladd well?

200
00:14:47,120 --> 00:14:48,097
Not really.

201
00:14:48,121 --> 00:14:49,331
She liked to keep to herself.

202
00:14:49,355 --> 00:14:51,500
Mm-hmm.

203
00:14:51,524 --> 00:14:53,402
Do you know what
she did for a living?

204
00:14:53,426 --> 00:14:54,837
Yeah, she was a call girl.

205
00:14:54,861 --> 00:14:55,123
Yeah.

206
00:14:55,147 --> 00:14:57,272
Hey, she paid her rent
on the first of every month.

207
00:14:57,296 --> 00:14:59,675
Well, I guess that's
better than some.

208
00:14:59,699 --> 00:15:01,310
Oh, hell, I'm late for a
doctor's appointment.

209
00:15:01,334 --> 00:15:02,411
Listen, can you do me a favor

210
00:15:02,435 --> 00:15:03,512
and lock the door
when you leave?

211
00:15:03,536 --> 00:15:04,747
- Yeah.
- Great, thanks.

212
00:15:04,771 --> 00:15:07,383
It's not here, Ben.

213
00:15:07,407 --> 00:15:09,151
Uh, well, we'll go with you.

214
00:15:09,175 --> 00:15:10,175
Go with you.

215
00:15:11,177 --> 00:15:13,222
Well, Conrad...

216
00:15:13,246 --> 00:15:14,456
looks like you're going to pop

217
00:15:14,480 --> 00:15:17,314
for that steak and
potatoes yourself.

218
00:15:24,290 --> 00:15:28,003
Uh, did you call the police
the night she was killed?

219
00:15:28,027 --> 00:15:29,004
No, I wasn't here.

220
00:15:29,028 --> 00:15:30,272
I was at a family dinner.

221
00:15:30,296 --> 00:15:32,608
By the time I got back, the
police had already shown up.

222
00:15:32,632 --> 00:15:33,742
Oh, well...

223
00:15:33,766 --> 00:15:35,177
- Good luck to you, Mr. Matlock.
- Thanks.

224
00:15:35,201 --> 00:15:37,112
- Sure.
- Thanks.

225
00:15:40,273 --> 00:15:41,417
Huh?

226
00:15:41,441 --> 00:15:44,787
Tom Marshall's
not on this registry.

227
00:15:44,811 --> 00:15:46,789
Maybe he lives somewhere else.

228
00:15:46,813 --> 00:15:49,324
Superintendent lives
in apartment 101.

229
00:15:49,348 --> 00:15:51,544
His name's D. Harper.

230
00:16:24,217 --> 00:16:26,209
Excuse me, I'm looking
for Dr. David Campbell.

231
00:16:27,253 --> 00:16:29,398
I'm Dr. Campbell.

232
00:16:29,422 --> 00:16:31,414
Oh.

233
00:16:33,259 --> 00:16:36,104
Michelle Thomas, Doctor...
Ben Matlock's associate.

234
00:16:36,128 --> 00:16:37,406
We're representing Ann Rawls.

235
00:16:37,430 --> 00:16:38,607
Right, right.

236
00:16:38,631 --> 00:16:40,642
This is Bert Ginsberg,
my station manager.

237
00:16:40,666 --> 00:16:41,844
- Miss Thomas.
- Hello.

238
00:16:41,868 --> 00:16:43,579
- Why don't we talk in my office?
- Okay.

239
00:16:43,603 --> 00:16:45,647
- Bert, I'll see you at 7:00?
- You got it.

240
00:16:50,843 --> 00:16:52,821
Thank you.

241
00:16:52,845 --> 00:16:55,246
- You care for some coffee?
- No, thanks.

242
00:16:58,384 --> 00:17:01,997
I take it Ann Rawls
told you about the tape.

243
00:17:02,021 --> 00:17:03,265
Yes, she did.

244
00:17:03,289 --> 00:17:05,467
You must think
quite poorly of me.

245
00:17:05,491 --> 00:17:07,870
Well, I try not to judge
people, Dr. Campbell.

246
00:17:07,894 --> 00:17:10,005
I'm sure you must have
been pretty frightened

247
00:17:10,029 --> 00:17:11,306
when Janie told you

248
00:17:11,330 --> 00:17:14,095
that your relationship with
her might be made public.

249
00:17:16,135 --> 00:17:19,882
Miss Thomas...

250
00:17:19,906 --> 00:17:23,552
I... am not capable of violence.

251
00:17:23,576 --> 00:17:26,021
Where were you at 6:30

252
00:17:26,045 --> 00:17:28,290
the evening Janie was
killed, Dr. Campbell?

253
00:17:28,314 --> 00:17:29,391
Home.

254
00:17:29,415 --> 00:17:30,826
Alone?

255
00:17:30,850 --> 00:17:33,562
Yes, I'm afraid so.

256
00:17:33,586 --> 00:17:37,599
My wife is an actress...
Blaine Campbell.

257
00:17:37,623 --> 00:17:39,268
She's been on the road
for the last couple months

258
00:17:39,292 --> 00:17:41,937
in a touring production.

259
00:17:41,961 --> 00:17:43,338
Well, did you talk to anyone

260
00:17:43,362 --> 00:17:46,008
who might be able to confirm
that you were home that evening?

261
00:17:46,032 --> 00:17:48,343
Yes, I did, I was on the
phone most of the night.

262
00:17:48,367 --> 00:17:49,344
With whom?

263
00:17:49,368 --> 00:17:51,313
Some of my private patients.

264
00:17:51,337 --> 00:17:53,482
I have a full schedule
of patients that I see.

265
00:17:53,506 --> 00:17:54,483
Well, do you remember the person

266
00:17:54,507 --> 00:17:55,918
you were speaking
to around 6:30?

267
00:17:55,942 --> 00:17:57,352
Yes, I think so.

268
00:17:57,376 --> 00:17:59,288
May I have the name, please?

269
00:17:59,312 --> 00:18:00,756
No.

270
00:18:00,780 --> 00:18:03,625
Excuse me?

271
00:18:03,649 --> 00:18:05,861
Miss Thomas, I can't
divulge the name of a patient

272
00:18:05,885 --> 00:18:07,930
without his permission.

273
00:18:07,954 --> 00:18:10,866
It would be both unethical
and unprofessional of me.

274
00:18:10,890 --> 00:18:11,967
I don't anticipate a problem,

275
00:18:11,991 --> 00:18:13,602
but I'll have to
talk to him first

276
00:18:13,626 --> 00:18:15,094
and then I'll get back to you.

277
00:18:20,232 --> 00:18:23,111
I'll get it.

278
00:18:29,575 --> 00:18:30,953
Hi.

279
00:18:30,977 --> 00:18:32,821
Oh, hi, hi.

280
00:18:32,845 --> 00:18:35,057
Co-Co-C-Come in, come in.

281
00:18:35,081 --> 00:18:39,628
Uh, uh, uh... Miss Hawkins,

282
00:18:39,652 --> 00:18:41,463
this is, uh, Madam Rawls.

283
00:18:42,688 --> 00:18:43,865
<i>Miss Rawls.</i>

284
00:18:43,889 --> 00:18:46,435
How do you do?

285
00:18:46,459 --> 00:18:47,603
Miss Hawkins.

286
00:18:47,627 --> 00:18:49,438
Thank you.

287
00:18:49,462 --> 00:18:51,573
That'll be all?

288
00:18:51,597 --> 00:18:52,826
Yes.

289
00:18:53,866 --> 00:18:56,631
All right.

290
00:19:01,641 --> 00:19:03,018
Come on in.

291
00:19:03,042 --> 00:19:05,587
What, what, what
brings you way out here?

292
00:19:05,611 --> 00:19:10,192
Well, now, I had a
message that you called...

293
00:19:10,216 --> 00:19:13,629
Something about
"you had good news."

294
00:19:13,653 --> 00:19:17,199
- Hmm.
- What's the good news?

295
00:19:17,223 --> 00:19:21,803
Oh, oh, oh, th-the, the judge
who was assigned to your case

296
00:19:21,827 --> 00:19:25,841
had to step down
because of illness.

297
00:19:25,865 --> 00:19:28,243
Why is that good news?

298
00:19:28,267 --> 00:19:33,215
Well, Judge Harris would
hang a jaywalker if he could.

299
00:19:33,239 --> 00:19:36,051
He'd make our lives miserable.

300
00:19:36,075 --> 00:19:38,220
Ken Harris.

301
00:19:38,244 --> 00:19:40,338
You know him?

302
00:19:42,081 --> 00:19:44,292
He's a client?

303
00:19:44,316 --> 00:19:45,894
Judge Harris?

304
00:19:45,918 --> 00:19:48,830
You'd think he'd smile more.

305
00:19:50,423 --> 00:19:52,067
DMV just got back to me.

306
00:19:52,091 --> 00:19:53,969
You know that car Tom
Marshall was driving?

307
00:19:53,993 --> 00:19:55,170
Yeah?

308
00:19:55,194 --> 00:19:56,304
It was rented.

309
00:19:56,328 --> 00:19:58,807
Yeah, by whom?

310
00:19:58,831 --> 00:20:02,144
A Mark Evans.

311
00:20:02,168 --> 00:20:03,745
Is that supposed to ring a bell?

312
00:20:03,769 --> 00:20:08,207
No, but his boss' name
should... Senator Jeffrey Paul.

313
00:20:09,241 --> 00:20:11,687
Excuse me?

314
00:20:11,711 --> 00:20:13,188
Well, well, well.

315
00:20:13,212 --> 00:20:15,424
Well, well, well, well, well.

316
00:20:15,448 --> 00:20:18,260
More and more good
news today, huh?

317
00:20:18,284 --> 00:20:22,664
Hmm... I bet you're going
to have to get right on this.

318
00:20:22,688 --> 00:20:25,867
Oh, you betcha.

319
00:20:25,891 --> 00:20:29,071
Well, I guess I'll just go.

320
00:20:29,095 --> 00:20:30,906
Okay.

321
00:20:31,931 --> 00:20:36,027
Uh... but... you know...

322
00:20:37,269 --> 00:20:39,514
you and I could
have dinner tonight.

323
00:20:39,538 --> 00:20:42,350
I mean, you do
have a rain check.

324
00:20:43,375 --> 00:20:46,755
Uh, well, well, I...

325
00:20:46,779 --> 00:20:50,859
I ate such a late lunch that,

326
00:20:50,883 --> 00:20:55,831
that I-I-I don't think I'd be
hungry for-for-for dinner.

327
00:20:55,855 --> 00:20:57,933
Oh.

328
00:20:57,957 --> 00:21:01,770
Well, I guess I'll just have
to catch you another time.

329
00:21:03,496 --> 00:21:07,375
When you're... hungry.

330
00:21:11,003 --> 00:21:13,815
À bientôt.

331
00:21:13,839 --> 00:21:18,453
À-À bi... à bi...
à bi... À bientôt.

332
00:21:31,590 --> 00:21:34,136
One thing I know
about my constituents...

333
00:21:34,160 --> 00:21:35,937
If I was a gambler or a drunk,

334
00:21:35,961 --> 00:21:38,507
there'd be all kinds
of compassion.

335
00:21:38,531 --> 00:21:40,876
Yeah, but they find out
you're seeing a call girl,

336
00:21:40,900 --> 00:21:42,511
you might as well
pack up the office...

337
00:21:42,535 --> 00:21:43,545
And head for the hills.

338
00:21:43,569 --> 00:21:44,679
Yeah.

339
00:21:44,703 --> 00:21:47,616
Did your young man find
what he was looking for

340
00:21:47,640 --> 00:21:49,632
over at Janie's
apartment the other day?

341
00:21:51,677 --> 00:21:54,272
Yes.

342
00:21:58,517 --> 00:22:00,179
It's all yours.

343
00:22:01,220 --> 00:22:03,799
You're giving it to me?

344
00:22:03,823 --> 00:22:05,367
Well, isn't that
why you're here?

345
00:22:05,391 --> 00:22:08,136
Well, it was on my list, yeah.

346
00:22:09,461 --> 00:22:12,140
Look, Ben, I know
I'm still a suspect,

347
00:22:12,164 --> 00:22:18,013
but I do have an alibi, so
I figured that if I help you,

348
00:22:18,037 --> 00:22:21,116
you will try to keep
my name out of the trial.

349
00:22:21,140 --> 00:22:23,185
Do we have a deal?

350
00:22:23,209 --> 00:22:28,190
Well, it depends on
how airtight your alibi is.

351
00:22:28,214 --> 00:22:33,128
Well, when this murder
allegedly occurred,

352
00:22:33,152 --> 00:22:37,055
I was being interviewed
on Channel Four... live.

353
00:22:38,090 --> 00:22:40,068
Airtight?

354
00:22:40,092 --> 00:22:42,186
Vacuum sealed.

355
00:22:44,230 --> 00:22:46,408
Look, Ben, I think there's
something on this tape

356
00:22:46,432 --> 00:22:48,043
that you should listen to.

357
00:22:48,067 --> 00:22:52,647
Uh, did you know that one of
Janie's clients was a minister?

358
00:22:52,671 --> 00:22:54,616
Reverend Morley Phelps.

359
00:22:54,640 --> 00:22:55,884
Yeah, well, it appears

360
00:22:55,908 --> 00:23:01,423
that, uh, Reverend
Phelps had quite a secret.

361
00:23:01,447 --> 00:23:04,259
How many times must I
apologize to you, Janie?

362
00:23:04,283 --> 00:23:07,262
Apologies aren't going to
bring back my sister, Reverend.

363
00:23:07,286 --> 00:23:08,930
<i>You can't hold me responsible</i>

364
00:23:08,954 --> 00:23:11,366
<i>for what happened to her.</i>

365
00:23:11,390 --> 00:23:14,189
<i>You're a murderer, a
cold-blooded murderer.</i>

366
00:23:41,620 --> 00:23:43,598
<i>I've done nothing wrong.</i>

367
00:23:43,622 --> 00:23:46,201
You were seeing Janie Ladd.

368
00:23:46,225 --> 00:23:47,769
What would your
congregation think

369
00:23:47,793 --> 00:23:51,072
if they found out about that?

370
00:23:51,096 --> 00:23:53,575
Janie Ladd was a human being.

371
00:23:53,599 --> 00:23:55,443
I feel a great deal of
shame and remorse

372
00:23:55,467 --> 00:23:58,246
about my relationship with her.

373
00:23:58,270 --> 00:24:01,883
Janie made an audiotape of a
conversation she had with you

374
00:24:01,907 --> 00:24:04,386
and she was using
it to blackmail you

375
00:24:04,410 --> 00:24:08,074
into talking to Ann
Rawls on her behalf.

376
00:24:09,248 --> 00:24:11,960
What did she think
you did to her sister?

377
00:24:11,984 --> 00:24:14,029
I don't have to answer that.

378
00:24:14,053 --> 00:24:16,498
She accused you of
murdering her sister.

379
00:24:16,522 --> 00:24:18,133
That's a very serious charge.

380
00:24:18,157 --> 00:24:21,136
If I were you, I'd
want to clarify that.

381
00:24:21,160 --> 00:24:22,737
You're not saying I killed her?

382
00:24:22,761 --> 00:24:26,664
Mr. Matlock, I am a man of God.

383
00:24:28,767 --> 00:24:30,812
I'm sorry, Reverend.

384
00:24:30,836 --> 00:24:34,115
I'll have to ask you
where you were that night.

385
00:24:34,139 --> 00:24:37,285
I was here, reading Scripture.

386
00:24:37,309 --> 00:24:39,574
As God is my witness.

387
00:24:42,581 --> 00:24:44,526
<i>You called today,
wanting to know</i>

388
00:24:44,550 --> 00:24:46,561
<i>about Janie Ladd's sister.</i>

389
00:24:46,585 --> 00:24:50,532
Uh, she died of a drug
overdose about ten years ago.

390
00:24:50,556 --> 00:24:52,200
- Um...
- Suicide?

391
00:24:52,224 --> 00:24:54,869
- Yeah, that's what Janie said.
- Oh.

392
00:24:54,893 --> 00:24:59,074
Well, thank you for
telling me about it.

393
00:24:59,098 --> 00:25:00,775
But you...

394
00:25:00,799 --> 00:25:03,845
you didn't have to make a
whole special trip out here.

395
00:25:03,869 --> 00:25:06,114
I like to come here.

396
00:25:06,138 --> 00:25:08,130
Oh.

397
00:25:09,541 --> 00:25:11,019
You look real tired.

398
00:25:11,043 --> 00:25:12,754
- Mmm.
- Long day?

399
00:25:12,778 --> 00:25:16,715
Long, long... long day.

400
00:25:18,083 --> 00:25:19,961
Stiff neck?

401
00:25:19,985 --> 00:25:22,797
Yeah. Mm-hmm. Yeah.

402
00:25:22,821 --> 00:25:24,813
Let me help.

403
00:25:27,526 --> 00:25:29,771
Ooh.

404
00:25:29,795 --> 00:25:32,560
You are tight.

405
00:25:34,500 --> 00:25:36,044
Relax.

406
00:25:36,068 --> 00:25:37,730
Come on, loosen up.

407
00:25:41,140 --> 00:25:43,132
Put your head back.

408
00:25:50,416 --> 00:25:52,193
Mmm.

409
00:25:52,217 --> 00:25:54,029
Now, how does that feel?

410
00:25:54,053 --> 00:25:56,818
Ah... feels good.

411
00:25:59,124 --> 00:26:01,870
Take your shirt off, and...

412
00:26:01,894 --> 00:26:04,159
I'll do your back.

413
00:26:05,864 --> 00:26:07,842
Uh... oh, no.

414
00:26:07,866 --> 00:26:09,044
No, no.

415
00:26:09,068 --> 00:26:11,946
Boy, I feel... better already.

416
00:26:11,970 --> 00:26:13,715
Yes.

417
00:26:13,739 --> 00:26:16,284
And it's getting
really late, and my...

418
00:26:16,308 --> 00:26:18,319
boy, my neck feels better.

419
00:26:18,343 --> 00:26:20,989
Well, uh, maybe you can rest.

420
00:26:21,013 --> 00:26:23,058
- Yeah.
- Mmm.

421
00:26:23,082 --> 00:26:25,460
- Yeah.
- Well, next time,

422
00:26:25,484 --> 00:26:27,762
as the saying goes, -

423
00:26:27,786 --> 00:26:31,052
will it be your place or mine?

424
00:26:32,091 --> 00:26:33,787
What are you talking about?

425
00:26:34,927 --> 00:26:37,038
- Don't be so innocent.
- Miss Rawls,

426
00:26:37,062 --> 00:26:41,309
it was never my intention
that... that this be anything

427
00:26:41,333 --> 00:26:44,312
except a strictly
business relationship.

428
00:26:44,336 --> 00:26:46,881
Oh, I know.

429
00:26:46,905 --> 00:26:50,018
But it happens all the time.

430
00:26:50,042 --> 00:26:55,423
It's not unusual for
a client to fall in love

431
00:26:55,447 --> 00:26:57,439
with her attorney.

432
00:26:59,251 --> 00:27:01,496
Are you saying that you're...

433
00:27:01,520 --> 00:27:04,513
in love with me?

434
00:27:05,557 --> 00:27:07,969
Well, not yet.

435
00:27:07,993 --> 00:27:10,738
But I do like you.

436
00:27:10,762 --> 00:27:13,608
I like you an awful lot, Benji.

437
00:27:13,632 --> 00:27:16,077
Oh, this is crazy.

438
00:27:16,101 --> 00:27:19,180
This is just...
this is just crazy.

439
00:27:19,204 --> 00:27:20,548
Why?

440
00:27:20,572 --> 00:27:22,784
Why can't you and I be friends?

441
00:27:22,808 --> 00:27:26,654
I mean, really good friends.

442
00:27:26,678 --> 00:27:29,457
Well, for one... for
one thing, I, uh...

443
00:27:29,481 --> 00:27:32,961
I don't particularly like
what you do for a living.

444
00:27:32,985 --> 00:27:35,363
I could give it up.

445
00:27:35,387 --> 00:27:36,464
What?

446
00:27:36,488 --> 00:27:38,466
Well, I always said that <i>if</i>

447
00:27:38,490 --> 00:27:41,603
the right man came
along, I could give it up.

448
00:27:41,627 --> 00:27:45,340
Oh, no, this is crazy.

449
00:27:45,364 --> 00:27:46,808
No.

450
00:27:46,832 --> 00:27:48,343
- No, it's crazy.
- Well,

451
00:27:48,367 --> 00:27:51,579
- No, it's...
- crazy as it sounds,

452
00:27:51,603 --> 00:27:55,836
I could really make you happy.

453
00:27:58,510 --> 00:28:00,388
What do you say?

454
00:28:00,412 --> 00:28:02,924
Think about it, Benji.

455
00:28:02,948 --> 00:28:05,008
Okay?

456
00:28:07,653 --> 00:28:10,179
Don't call me Benji.

457
00:28:11,857 --> 00:28:15,136
Wasn't that the name
of a dog in a movie?

458
00:28:15,160 --> 00:28:16,992
Mm-hmm.

459
00:28:18,430 --> 00:28:20,331
Dog!

460
00:28:33,879 --> 00:28:37,077
You have two minutes, and
then I want you out of here.

461
00:28:41,420 --> 00:28:44,732
Dominick, this is Miss Thomas,
the lawyer I told you about.

462
00:28:44,756 --> 00:28:46,100
Nice to meet you.

463
00:28:46,124 --> 00:28:48,603
I know this is an invasion
of your privacy, Mr. Sinclair...

464
00:28:48,627 --> 00:28:49,938
I apologize for that...

465
00:28:49,962 --> 00:28:51,806
But I just need to
confirm a few things.

466
00:28:51,830 --> 00:28:53,007
I understand.

467
00:28:53,031 --> 00:28:54,475
I've apprised Dominick
of the situation.

468
00:28:54,499 --> 00:28:56,491
Why don't you sit down?

469
00:28:59,071 --> 00:29:02,083
Mr. Sinclair, you're a private
patient of Dr. Campbell's?

470
00:29:02,107 --> 00:29:03,084
Yes, I am.

471
00:29:03,108 --> 00:29:04,652
I need to ask you
a few questions

472
00:29:04,676 --> 00:29:06,521
about the evening of June 10.

473
00:29:06,545 --> 00:29:08,523
Do you remember how
you spent that evening?

474
00:29:08,547 --> 00:29:10,925
Yes, I was at home all
evening with my wife.

475
00:29:10,949 --> 00:29:13,094
Do you recall speaking
with Dr. Campbell?

476
00:29:13,118 --> 00:29:14,495
Quite clearly.

477
00:29:14,519 --> 00:29:16,598
We spoke for the
better part of 20 minutes.

478
00:29:16,622 --> 00:29:18,733
- What time was that?
- 6:30.

479
00:29:18,757 --> 00:29:20,435
Could it possibly have been...

480
00:29:20,459 --> 00:29:23,238
6:15? Or maybe later... 6:45?

481
00:29:23,262 --> 00:29:25,273
No, I have a favorite news show

482
00:29:25,297 --> 00:29:27,775
that I watch on Thursdays,
and it ends at 6:30.

483
00:29:27,799 --> 00:29:30,928
I remember Dr. Campbell
called right after I turned it off.

484
00:29:32,471 --> 00:29:34,782
Well, I guess that
just about covers it.

485
00:29:34,806 --> 00:29:37,385
Thank you for your
time, Mr. Sinclair.

486
00:29:37,409 --> 00:29:38,920
I'll walk you out.

487
00:29:38,944 --> 00:29:40,936
Dominick, one second.

488
00:29:46,151 --> 00:29:48,620
<i>Well, Conrad...</i>

489
00:29:50,322 --> 00:29:52,200
I believe we're closing in.

490
00:29:52,224 --> 00:29:56,738
Yes, sir. I believe
we're closing in.

491
00:29:58,030 --> 00:29:59,474
You think it's the
preacher, huh?

492
00:29:59,498 --> 00:30:03,111
Yeah, I'm kind of sorry,
but I think it's the preacher.

493
00:30:05,137 --> 00:30:08,182
Uh, Miss Hawkins,
would you get that?

494
00:30:08,206 --> 00:30:09,817
You want me to go with you?

495
00:30:09,841 --> 00:30:11,776
No, I'll handle it.

496
00:30:13,345 --> 00:30:15,507
Oh, thank you.

497
00:30:18,550 --> 00:30:20,280
This just came.

498
00:30:21,820 --> 00:30:23,531
Yeah?

499
00:30:23,555 --> 00:30:24,866
Huh.

500
00:30:24,890 --> 00:30:26,534
Thanks.

501
00:30:26,558 --> 00:30:28,169
What in the world?

502
00:30:29,828 --> 00:30:31,854
Ah.

503
00:30:33,098 --> 00:30:34,442
Oh.

504
00:30:34,466 --> 00:30:36,210
Pajamas.

505
00:30:36,234 --> 00:30:38,079
And silk.

506
00:30:38,103 --> 00:30:40,615
Yeah. Well, who in the world...?

507
00:30:45,644 --> 00:30:49,057
"Benji, I have a matching pair.

508
00:30:49,081 --> 00:30:52,460
"Wouldn't it be nice
if they got together?

509
00:30:52,484 --> 00:30:54,476
Ann."

510
00:30:57,789 --> 00:31:00,588
You tell anybody
about this, I'll kill you.

511
00:31:30,555 --> 00:31:32,547
♪♪

512
00:31:35,594 --> 00:31:38,272
Auggie, Ben Matlock.

513
00:31:38,296 --> 00:31:41,789
Tell Bob Brooks I got to
speak with him right away.

514
00:32:13,331 --> 00:32:14,809
Haven't you
anything better to do

515
00:32:14,833 --> 00:32:16,778
than follow me
around, Mr. Matlock?

516
00:32:16,802 --> 00:32:18,794
Well...

517
00:32:20,305 --> 00:32:23,742
What, uh... what
are you doing here?

518
00:32:24,876 --> 00:32:27,388
I'm packing some
of Janie's things.

519
00:32:27,412 --> 00:32:29,904
Do the police know
you have a key?

520
00:32:33,218 --> 00:32:35,863
Okay, Mr. Matlock.

521
00:32:35,887 --> 00:32:39,934
You want to know how
I got a key to this place.

522
00:32:39,958 --> 00:32:44,405
I got a key from the only
person Janie gave one to.

523
00:32:44,429 --> 00:32:45,807
She lives in Italy.

524
00:32:45,831 --> 00:32:48,009
She does now. She didn't always.

525
00:32:48,033 --> 00:32:50,935
What do you know
about Janie's mother?

526
00:32:51,970 --> 00:32:53,598
I was married to her.

527
00:32:56,475 --> 00:32:58,774
Janie was your daughter?

528
00:33:00,045 --> 00:33:01,980
As was Pamela.

529
00:33:03,748 --> 00:33:06,445
Why didn't you
tell me this before?

530
00:33:12,724 --> 00:33:15,193
I'm a minister, Mr. Matlock.

531
00:33:16,828 --> 00:33:19,941
What am I supposed to tell you?

532
00:33:19,965 --> 00:33:22,944
That my... my daughter, who...
who happened to make a living

533
00:33:22,968 --> 00:33:25,279
sleeping with men,
hated me so bad

534
00:33:25,303 --> 00:33:29,240
that she made me pay her
$300 an hour just to talk to her?

535
00:33:31,376 --> 00:33:34,055
Why did she hate you?

536
00:33:34,079 --> 00:33:37,277
When my daughters
were growing up, I drank.

537
00:33:38,316 --> 00:33:40,649
I was violent, abusive.

538
00:33:43,455 --> 00:33:48,970
One day, when Janie
was 16 and Pamela was 14,

539
00:33:48,994 --> 00:33:54,408
I came home, and my wife
and daughters were gone.

540
00:33:54,432 --> 00:33:57,111
Her lawyer handed
me a restraining order.

541
00:33:57,135 --> 00:34:01,402
That insured that I'd never
go around them again.

542
00:34:08,346 --> 00:34:11,492
18 months later, my
ex-wife called me and said

543
00:34:11,516 --> 00:34:15,429
that Pamela had swallowed
a bottle of sleeping pills.

544
00:34:15,453 --> 00:34:17,598
And Janie blamed you.

545
00:34:17,622 --> 00:34:21,569
She swore she'd
never talk to me again.

546
00:34:21,593 --> 00:34:26,361
And then, four years
ago, my entire life changed.

547
00:34:28,133 --> 00:34:29,726
I found God.

548
00:34:30,769 --> 00:34:33,180
I embraced the church.

549
00:34:33,204 --> 00:34:35,483
I became a minister.

550
00:34:35,507 --> 00:34:40,946
And I made a personal commitment
to make things right with Janie.

551
00:34:46,084 --> 00:34:50,920
Uh, she said that, uh,
if I wanted to see her...

552
00:34:53,725 --> 00:34:56,718
I'd, uh, have to
pay for her time...

553
00:34:58,730 --> 00:35:01,894
uh, just like every
other man in her life.

554
00:35:08,440 --> 00:35:10,518
Ben, everything all right?

555
00:35:10,542 --> 00:35:12,753
Uh, yeah. Yeah.

556
00:35:12,777 --> 00:35:14,956
B-Bob, I'm... I'm sorry
to get you out here.

557
00:35:14,980 --> 00:35:16,891
It was a false alarm.

558
00:35:16,915 --> 00:35:18,213
Wrong guy?

559
00:35:19,250 --> 00:35:21,879
Yeah.

560
00:35:36,434 --> 00:35:38,045
Mr. Ginsberg.

561
00:35:38,069 --> 00:35:40,214
Ah. Miss, um... Thomas.

562
00:35:40,238 --> 00:35:41,849
- Yes.
- Yeah, what can I do for you?

563
00:35:41,873 --> 00:35:43,317
Would you happen
to remember if you

564
00:35:43,341 --> 00:35:45,186
were working here
the evening of June 10?

565
00:35:45,210 --> 00:35:46,187
It was a Thursday.

566
00:35:46,211 --> 00:35:47,588
Oh, I'm here every Thursday.

567
00:35:47,612 --> 00:35:49,490
Well, was Dr. Campbell
working that evening?

568
00:35:49,514 --> 00:35:50,992
Sure. He's never missed a show.

569
00:35:51,016 --> 00:35:52,660
Although, he did miss
our dinner that night.

570
00:35:52,684 --> 00:35:54,028
He went home with the flu.

571
00:35:54,052 --> 00:35:55,696
Uh, every Thursday
after the show,

572
00:35:55,720 --> 00:35:58,366
we have dinner to
rehash the week.

573
00:35:58,390 --> 00:36:01,035
But that Thursday,
Dr. Campbell went home sick.

574
00:36:01,059 --> 00:36:04,338
Yeah, so I got to,
uh, order in Chinese

575
00:36:04,362 --> 00:36:06,607
and watch the Braves
game on television.

576
00:36:06,631 --> 00:36:08,943
I called him between
innings to see how he was.

577
00:36:08,967 --> 00:36:10,144
And how was he?

578
00:36:10,168 --> 00:36:11,659
Beats me.

579
00:36:13,938 --> 00:36:16,017
<i>It's hard to imagine, Ben.</i>

580
00:36:16,041 --> 00:36:19,420
A daughter charging her
father money just to talk with her.

581
00:36:19,444 --> 00:36:20,621
Yeah.

582
00:36:20,645 --> 00:36:21,989
You ready?

583
00:36:22,013 --> 00:36:24,825
Yeah. Uh, could I use the phone?

584
00:36:24,849 --> 00:36:26,961
I need to see if
Conrad's looking for me.

585
00:36:26,985 --> 00:36:28,629
Sure. There's a
portable in the kitchen.

586
00:36:28,653 --> 00:36:30,631
Yeah. Okay.

587
00:36:32,457 --> 00:36:33,801
Oh.

588
00:36:33,825 --> 00:36:35,054
Um...

589
00:36:38,463 --> 00:36:39,607
Oh.

590
00:36:41,232 --> 00:36:42,791
Oh.

591
00:36:55,647 --> 00:36:57,625
<i>Dr. Campbell,</i>

592
00:36:57,649 --> 00:37:01,395
I understand that your talk show

593
00:37:01,419 --> 00:37:05,800
is one of the most popular
radio programs in all of Atlanta.

594
00:37:05,824 --> 00:37:07,835
We have a very loyal following.

595
00:37:07,859 --> 00:37:12,239
Boy, the-the calls your
listeners, uh, send in

596
00:37:12,263 --> 00:37:14,475
for all the world to hear.

597
00:37:14,499 --> 00:37:16,510
I mean, uh, drug problems,

598
00:37:16,534 --> 00:37:18,546
sexual problems,
marital problems.

599
00:37:18,570 --> 00:37:20,614
They must have a
lot of respect for you.

600
00:37:20,638 --> 00:37:22,149
I certainly hope so.

601
00:37:22,173 --> 00:37:23,751
I guess that's, uh...

602
00:37:23,775 --> 00:37:26,220
that's, uh, very important in
your line of work... respect.

603
00:37:26,244 --> 00:37:29,056
I mean, if-if a patient
didn't have respect

604
00:37:29,080 --> 00:37:31,759
for his psychologist
and his opinion,

605
00:37:31,783 --> 00:37:34,395
I guess he might as well
just go somewheres else then.

606
00:37:34,419 --> 00:37:37,465
I have suggested that
on several occasions.

607
00:37:37,489 --> 00:37:38,966
Yeah.

608
00:37:38,990 --> 00:37:44,805
Uh, Doctor, uh... did
you know Janie Ladd?

609
00:37:44,829 --> 00:37:47,508
Yes, I did.

610
00:37:47,532 --> 00:37:50,969
How would you describe
your relationship with her?

611
00:37:53,004 --> 00:37:54,715
We were friends.

612
00:37:54,739 --> 00:37:57,785
Mm-hmm. Actually, you paid her

613
00:37:57,809 --> 00:38:00,554
to have sexual
relations with you

614
00:38:00,578 --> 00:38:02,223
for over two years, didn't you?

615
00:38:02,247 --> 00:38:05,526
Yes, that is true,
but we were friends.

616
00:38:05,550 --> 00:38:07,027
Hmm.

617
00:38:07,051 --> 00:38:11,265
Dr. Campbell, you're a
married man <i>and</i> a psychologist.

618
00:38:11,289 --> 00:38:13,267
When your patients find out

619
00:38:13,291 --> 00:38:16,570
that this man that they
held in such high regard

620
00:38:16,594 --> 00:38:18,773
was not only an adulterer,

621
00:38:18,797 --> 00:38:20,908
but solicited prostitutes,

622
00:38:20,932 --> 00:38:23,544
that's gonna knock a hole
right in your business, isn't it?

623
00:38:23,568 --> 00:38:24,879
Objection.

624
00:38:24,903 --> 00:38:26,580
Calls for speculation.

625
00:38:26,604 --> 00:38:28,149
Sustained.

626
00:38:28,173 --> 00:38:31,085
According to the, uh, coroner,

627
00:38:31,109 --> 00:38:34,054
Janie Ladd was killed around...

628
00:38:34,078 --> 00:38:37,525
6:30 on the evening
of June the tenth.

629
00:38:37,549 --> 00:38:39,226
Where were you at that time?

630
00:38:39,250 --> 00:38:41,428
I normally go out on Thursdays

631
00:38:41,452 --> 00:38:43,430
with the station
manager, Bert Ginsberg...

632
00:38:43,454 --> 00:38:44,732
But not that night?

633
00:38:44,756 --> 00:38:46,066
No. I wasn't feeling well,

634
00:38:46,090 --> 00:38:48,702
so I thought I should
get a good night's sleep.

635
00:38:48,726 --> 00:38:51,172
Oh, yeah. How far is your
house from the station?

636
00:38:51,196 --> 00:38:53,007
20 minutes.

637
00:38:53,031 --> 00:38:54,642
About 20 minutes. So about 6:20.

638
00:38:54,666 --> 00:38:56,277
- Mm.
- Did you go straight to bed?

639
00:38:56,301 --> 00:38:58,712
No. No. I, uh...

640
00:38:58,736 --> 00:39:00,514
I made some phone
calls to patients.

641
00:39:00,538 --> 00:39:02,616
Uh, I was on the
phone for quite a while.

642
00:39:02,640 --> 00:39:06,153
Oh. Oh, you remember
who you were on the phone to

643
00:39:06,177 --> 00:39:09,356
from say, oh, 6:30
to quarter to 7:00?

644
00:39:09,380 --> 00:39:11,892
Yes, I do. Dominick Sinclair.

645
00:39:11,916 --> 00:39:14,094
Mr. Sinclair was kind enough

646
00:39:14,118 --> 00:39:16,363
to authorize me
to reveal his name.

647
00:39:16,387 --> 00:39:17,565
Mm-hmm.

648
00:39:17,589 --> 00:39:19,667
And your associate
talked to him.

649
00:39:19,691 --> 00:39:22,169
Hmm. That's right,
and Mr. Sinclair

650
00:39:22,193 --> 00:39:25,573
did confirm that you
were on the phone with him

651
00:39:25,597 --> 00:39:26,874
at the time you said.

652
00:39:26,898 --> 00:39:28,175
I'm aware of that.

653
00:39:28,199 --> 00:39:29,610
Yeah, yeah. Yeah.

654
00:39:29,634 --> 00:39:32,279
Uh, his house is pretty
close to yours, isn't it?

655
00:39:32,303 --> 00:39:34,048
Well, just about
a couple blocks.

656
00:39:34,072 --> 00:39:37,051
Yeah, so, there'd be no
telephone record of that call

657
00:39:37,075 --> 00:39:39,010
'cause it's a local
call, isn't that right?

658
00:39:41,045 --> 00:39:45,426
Well... I mean, Dominick said
that he-he talked to me at 6:30.

659
00:39:45,450 --> 00:39:46,794
You know that already.

660
00:39:46,818 --> 00:39:48,829
Yeah, yeah, we-we know that.

661
00:39:48,853 --> 00:39:52,433
We know that you spoke
to him on <i>a</i> telephone.

662
00:39:52,457 --> 00:39:55,236
We don't know that
you spoke to him

663
00:39:55,260 --> 00:39:58,439
on <i>your</i> phone from <i>your</i> house.

664
00:39:58,463 --> 00:40:00,574
For crying out loud.

665
00:40:01,933 --> 00:40:03,911
You know, Mr. Ginsberg,

666
00:40:03,935 --> 00:40:07,448
your station manager at, uh...

667
00:40:07,472 --> 00:40:10,751
WRQE is a very caring man,

668
00:40:10,775 --> 00:40:13,254
and when you left
work sick that day,

669
00:40:13,278 --> 00:40:15,089
he was concerned
about your health,

670
00:40:15,113 --> 00:40:19,026
and he called you three times
to see how you were feeling.

671
00:40:19,050 --> 00:40:21,795
And here...

672
00:40:21,819 --> 00:40:25,532
Here's the telephone
bill... Blown up, of course...

673
00:40:25,556 --> 00:40:29,270
For the radio station
for the month of June.

674
00:40:29,294 --> 00:40:30,471
I've circled three numbers.

675
00:40:30,495 --> 00:40:31,705
They're all the same.

676
00:40:31,729 --> 00:40:33,274
You recognize that number?

677
00:40:33,298 --> 00:40:35,509
It's my home number.

678
00:40:35,533 --> 00:40:37,544
Yeah. Yeah.

679
00:40:37,568 --> 00:40:42,516
And Mr. Ginsberg
is prepared to testify

680
00:40:42,540 --> 00:40:46,220
that he called that number
three times that evening,

681
00:40:46,244 --> 00:40:51,425
and each time, he got your
answer machine and hung up.

682
00:40:51,449 --> 00:40:52,826
Why wasn't he able to get you?

683
00:40:52,850 --> 00:40:54,028
You have only the one line

684
00:40:54,052 --> 00:40:55,229
coming into your
house, don't you?

685
00:40:55,253 --> 00:40:57,197
Yes. Uh...

686
00:40:57,221 --> 00:41:00,334
Yeah, but something's looking
very, very wrong here, see.

687
00:41:00,358 --> 00:41:01,435
Look at the times.

688
00:41:01,459 --> 00:41:02,603
Something's very
wrong. Look here.

689
00:41:02,627 --> 00:41:07,975
He called at 6:32
p.m., 6:44, 6:56.

690
00:41:07,999 --> 00:41:12,646
I mean, if you... If
you were on the phone

691
00:41:12,670 --> 00:41:17,451
from, say, uh,
6:30 to almost 7:00,

692
00:41:17,475 --> 00:41:20,521
just the one line
coming into your house,

693
00:41:20,545 --> 00:41:22,571
shouldn't he have
gotten a busy signal?

694
00:41:24,849 --> 00:41:26,427
I don't know. Maybe, uh...

695
00:41:26,451 --> 00:41:29,596
Maybe Mr. Sinclair and I
were a little off about the time.

696
00:41:29,620 --> 00:41:31,432
I-I could have been
taking a shower.

697
00:41:31,456 --> 00:41:34,435
I always put the machine
on when I go into the shower.

698
00:41:34,459 --> 00:41:36,403
I don't think so.

699
00:41:36,427 --> 00:41:39,907
I think you called
him at exactly 6:30,

700
00:41:39,931 --> 00:41:42,242
only you didn't call
him from your house.

701
00:41:42,266 --> 00:41:45,779
You called him from
Janie Ladd's apartment.

702
00:41:45,803 --> 00:41:53,153
- What?
- Your Honor... this telephone

703
00:41:53,177 --> 00:41:57,324
was taken from Janie
Ladd's apartment

704
00:41:57,348 --> 00:42:00,961
by Atlanta Police
Detective Bob Brooks

705
00:42:00,985 --> 00:42:02,262
with a warrant.

706
00:42:02,286 --> 00:42:06,600
I ask that it be marked
exhibit J for identification.

707
00:42:06,624 --> 00:42:08,002
It shall be marked.

708
00:42:08,026 --> 00:42:09,336
Thank you.

709
00:42:09,360 --> 00:42:12,306
Now, Dr. Campbell, I don't
know how familiar you are

710
00:42:12,330 --> 00:42:15,743
with these new portable phones

711
00:42:15,767 --> 00:42:17,978
and all of their...

712
00:42:18,002 --> 00:42:20,681
fancy little gadgets here.

713
00:42:20,705 --> 00:42:23,117
I mean, a lot of 'em...
I don't understand.

714
00:42:23,141 --> 00:42:24,451
Page... I got that here.

715
00:42:24,475 --> 00:42:28,122
And mute and-and
memory, and then,

716
00:42:28,146 --> 00:42:29,656
here's, uh... What's...?

717
00:42:29,680 --> 00:42:31,458
I-I got to get-get some glass...

718
00:42:31,482 --> 00:42:34,061
Wha-what's that
right there? See?

719
00:42:34,085 --> 00:42:35,763
Redial?

720
00:42:35,787 --> 00:42:36,964
Re-Redial. Now...

721
00:42:36,988 --> 00:42:39,433
Now what do you
suppose that function does?

722
00:42:39,457 --> 00:42:42,369
My wife never wanted to
get one of these new things.

723
00:42:42,393 --> 00:42:43,537
Yeah. Yeah.

724
00:42:43,561 --> 00:42:46,740
What it does... It
automatically calls

725
00:42:46,764 --> 00:42:49,791
the last number that was dialed.

726
00:42:51,135 --> 00:42:54,014
And, uh, uh, a few days ago,

727
00:42:54,038 --> 00:42:58,752
uh, Detective, uh,
Bob Brooks and I

728
00:42:58,776 --> 00:43:01,422
were at Janie Ladd's apartment,

729
00:43:01,446 --> 00:43:04,725
and I punched
this redial number.

730
00:43:06,984 --> 00:43:09,696
And guess what I got?

731
00:43:09,720 --> 00:43:14,249
555-7793.

732
00:43:16,194 --> 00:43:19,426
You know whose number that is?

733
00:43:22,800 --> 00:43:26,947
That's... Dominick
Sinclair's number.

734
00:43:26,971 --> 00:43:30,084
Now, how did Mr. Sinclair's
number get programmed

735
00:43:30,108 --> 00:43:32,907
into her telephone?

736
00:43:34,378 --> 00:43:35,689
I don't have any idea.

737
00:43:35,713 --> 00:43:37,858
You know... You
know, right-right after...

738
00:43:37,882 --> 00:43:39,960
shortly after she was killed,

739
00:43:39,984 --> 00:43:44,031
the police taped off
her whole apartment

740
00:43:44,055 --> 00:43:46,900
so that nobody could mess
with any of the evidence.

741
00:43:46,924 --> 00:43:48,836
And they put signs
on the telephones.

742
00:43:48,860 --> 00:43:50,471
"Do not use."

743
00:43:50,495 --> 00:43:53,006
You know what
that means, Doctor?

744
00:43:53,030 --> 00:43:57,044
That means, the last call

745
00:43:57,068 --> 00:44:00,714
that was made on this
phone before the police arrived

746
00:44:00,738 --> 00:44:03,584
was to Dominick Sinclair.

747
00:44:03,608 --> 00:44:05,819
How did his number
get in this telephone?

748
00:44:05,843 --> 00:44:08,055
He didn't know Miss Ladd.

749
00:44:08,079 --> 00:44:13,360
You made that call to
Mr. Sinclair on this telephone.

750
00:44:13,384 --> 00:44:17,231
You made that call after you
killed Janie Ladd, didn't you?

751
00:44:17,255 --> 00:44:18,765
No. That is absolutely not true.

752
00:44:18,789 --> 00:44:20,234
Oh, I think it is.

753
00:44:20,258 --> 00:44:22,369
You left your radio
station at 6:00,

754
00:44:22,393 --> 00:44:25,572
arrived at her apartment
some 20 minutes later,

755
00:44:25,596 --> 00:44:29,710
killed her, called
Dominick Sinclair,

756
00:44:29,734 --> 00:44:33,814
knowing full well that
calls from her apartment,

757
00:44:33,838 --> 00:44:39,153
your house to his
house, were all local calls,

758
00:44:39,177 --> 00:44:40,921
and the police
would never trace 'em

759
00:44:40,945 --> 00:44:43,090
'cause there wouldn't
be a record of them.

760
00:44:43,114 --> 00:44:48,462
Unfortunately for
you... there is a record.

761
00:44:48,486 --> 00:44:49,997
Mr. Sinclair

762
00:44:50,021 --> 00:44:51,798
is right here. He's not at home.

763
00:44:51,822 --> 00:44:55,102
He can't answer his phone.

764
00:44:55,126 --> 00:44:58,372
I'm gonna press
the redial button.

765
00:45:01,265 --> 00:45:02,709
Hello. This is
Dominick Sinclair.

766
00:45:02,733 --> 00:45:04,578
I can't come to the
phone right now,

767
00:45:04,602 --> 00:45:06,680
but if you'll leave a
message after the tone,

768
00:45:06,704 --> 00:45:08,949
I'll give you a call right back.

769
00:45:08,973 --> 00:45:11,101
The defense rests.

770
00:45:12,577 --> 00:45:16,623
We find the defendant,
Ann Rawls, not guilty.

771
00:45:16,647 --> 00:45:18,859
Thank you, ladies and
gentlemen of the jury.

772
00:45:18,883 --> 00:45:20,060
You're dismissed.

773
00:45:20,084 --> 00:45:22,196
This court is adjourned.

774
00:45:27,458 --> 00:45:29,770
Thank you, Benji.

775
00:45:29,794 --> 00:45:31,705
Call me.

776
00:45:31,729 --> 00:45:34,708
I am free this entire weekend.

777
00:45:34,732 --> 00:45:37,065
À bientôt.

778
00:45:40,538 --> 00:45:42,883
À bientôt.

779
00:45:42,907 --> 00:45:45,485
It's French.

780
00:45:45,509 --> 00:45:47,637
Means the check's in the mail.

9999
00:00:0,500 --> 00:00:2,00
